    {'heading': 'Lesson XXV'},
    {'para': '**268. Declension. Perfect Active Participles in ⟨vāṃs⟩.** The active participles of the perfect tense-system are quite peculiar as regards the modifications of the stem. In the strong cases the suffix is ⟨vāṃs⟩, which becomes ⟨vān⟩ in the nom. sing. masc., and is shortened to ⟨van⟩ in the voc. sing. In the weakest cases the suffix is contracted into ⟨uṣ⟩; and in the middle cases it is changed to ⟨vat⟩. A union-vowel ⟨i⟩, if present in the strong and middle cases, disappears before ⟨uṣ⟩ in the weakest. Radical ⟨i⟩ and ⟨ī⟩, if preceded by *one* consonant, become ⟨y⟩ before ⟨uṣ⟩, but if preceded by more than one consonant, become ⟨iy⟩; whereas radical ⟨u⟩ always becomes ⟨uv⟩ before ⟨uṣ⟩, and radical ⟨ṛ⟩, ⟨r⟩. Thus, ⟨ninīvāṃs⟩, ⟨ninyuṣ⟩; ⟨śuśruvāṃs⟩, ⟨śuśruvuṣ⟩; ⟨cakṛvāṃs⟩, ⟨cakruṣ⟩. The feminine stem is formed with ⟨ī⟩ from the weakest stem-form; thus, ⟨ninyuṣī⟩. Examples:'},
